WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.000
ROBERT SAXBY: Hi.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.080
Good afternoon.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:10.580
I'm Robert.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.930
I'm a big data specialist
based out of Amsterdam.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.540
I'm here today
together with Ashok.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.620
He's from the Bay Area.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:15.955
ASHOK HOLLA: Hello.

00:00:15.955 --> 00:00:17.329
ROBERT SAXBY: A
customer engineer

00:00:17.329 --> 00:00:18.570
working in the Bay Area.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.020
So we're going to take you
today through migrating

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.070
data warehouses.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.720
If you have a
question that really

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.645
can't wait until the end,
just put up your hand.

00:00:27.645 --> 00:00:28.770
There's going to be quite--

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.478
we're going to do this
in three sections.

00:00:30.478 --> 00:00:32.110
So the first 10,
15 minutes, we're

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.151
going to go through a
little bit of the reasoning

00:00:34.151 --> 00:00:37.802
why, then we're going to look
at some reference architectures.

00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:39.260
After we've done
that, we will move

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.300
on to a demo for
about 20 minutes

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.000
and actually look at some
of the work involved.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.370
And then we will come on
to the more, let's say,

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:47.480
the logistics of it.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.094
So we can go through
the technology.

00:00:50.094 --> 00:00:51.635
But after that, what
does it actually

00:00:51.635 --> 00:00:54.680
look like to actually get all
the parties involved actually

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.950
committed and working towards
the goal of this data warehouse

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.090
migration?

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.450
So to start us off,
let's have a little think

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.870
about a data warehouse
and what it actually is.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:09.384
So often, when we're talking
about data warehouses, when

00:01:09.384 --> 00:01:10.800
I'm working with
customers-- and I

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.710
should say previous to
joining Google myself,

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:14.780
I was a customer.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.180
I worked for a company
as a lead data engineer.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.124
And I moved a data
warehouse to BigQuery.

00:01:19.124 --> 00:01:20.540
So I have some
hands on experience

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.130
with the stuff as well.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.447
And when we were doing that,
often when I was talking

00:01:24.447 --> 00:01:26.030
to some of the people
in the business,

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.450
the use case that was presented
was the data warehouse.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.950
Well, the data warehouse
isn't a use case.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.730
It's something that
enables use cases.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.850
So it's the underlying platform.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.757
And that was really key
to get in everyone's minds

00:01:39.757 --> 00:01:42.340
to say, look, you know, there's
a reason why we're doing this.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.777
And let's understand what that
reason is, because then we

00:01:44.777 --> 00:01:46.610
can actually think about
what we want to do,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.515
because there might be some
stuff we want to migrate.

00:01:48.515 --> 00:01:50.480
There might be some
workloads that we no longer

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:51.460
want to take with us.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.290
But what are the use cases
we're trying to fulfill?

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.350
Now, we tried to split this
up into four different types

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:57.850
of use case.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:58.880
I'm going to walk
through them now.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.170
So here they all are--

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:04.070
operational reporting, ad hoc
reporting, ad hoc analysis,

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.510
and operationalized insight.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.970
So we'll take you
through one by one.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.400
So the first one,
operational reporting--

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.530
this is the common,
typical dashboarding area.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.080
So I'm in the business,
and I want a dashboard

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.320
to actually see how much
inventory do I have left,

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.750
how are the sales doing this
month, the queries get built,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.580
the dashboard gets put in place.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.530
So what are some of
the concerns that we

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:31.580
might want to think
about if we're

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:33.288
going to migrate some
of these workloads,

00:02:33.288 --> 00:02:36.080
or what are some of the concerns
that are typically associated

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.830
with this particular use case?

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.530
Well, one is obviously
data quality.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.690
If the data quality is not
good, those numbers aren't good.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.570
Data timeliness--
that's a big thing.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.790
Often when you're working
on premise with your data

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.290
warehouse team, especially if
you're coming from, let's say,

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.590
more legacy setup, there's
a lot of batch processes

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:55.730
that run at night.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.392
If one of those batch
processes fails,

00:02:57.392 --> 00:02:58.850
then the question
is, is everything

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.120
going to be there in time to
get the numbers up the next day?

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.000
I've heard that question
so many times in the past.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.850
And then reliability--
which obviously comes

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.320
into the other two as well.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.270
But if you don't have good
reliability with your system,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:13.940
then you're trying to--

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.170
the other two are very difficult
to deliver on, let's say.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.330
So next one is ad hoc reporting.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.180
So typically, with the
operational reporting,

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.040
that is the thing that everyone
says, I want those numbers.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.710
I want those
dashboards available.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.126
If they're not
there, you probably

00:03:29.126 --> 00:03:31.840
have some measurements
and metrics around there,

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.660
some SLOs that
you're trying to say

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.550
these dashboards are going to
be ready, available for everyone

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:38.690
to use.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:41.030
The ad hoc reporting
sometimes suffers

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.480
because it's resource bound.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.063
You have a certain
number of resources

00:03:44.063 --> 00:03:46.210
that you're using to
do all your processing.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:50.845
And then when it comes to the
actual ad hoc reporting piece,

00:03:50.845 --> 00:03:52.220
there are people
that have ideas.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.286
They want to try and
start looking at things.

00:03:54.286 --> 00:03:56.660
They're using visualization
tools and this kind of stuff.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.970
Essentially what they
need is more data.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.610
They need visualization
tools, and they need access

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.174
to historical data.

00:04:06.174 --> 00:04:08.090
So all of this requires
we have enough storage

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.280
to store the data, we
have enough capacity

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:12.050
to run those kind of
processing, and we

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:13.730
can support all the
different types of tools

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:14.646
that they want to use.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.890
So the next one is
ad hoc analysis.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:22.590
This is the data science
area, the statistical area

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.460
where people are
actually going in there.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.480
They're trying to work out
what's this new trend, what's

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.220
this thing that I'm looking at.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.750
Why have I got some
country, for example, that's

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.270
underperforming in sales?

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.950
It's been doing
well all the time,

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.533
and now suddenly
it's underperforming.

00:04:36.533 --> 00:04:37.290
What caused that?

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:40.080
And again, what we need
here is strong tools

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.190
to be able to do this.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.720
Again, capacity comes up.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.520
If you don't have the capacity,
then these types of questions

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.262
sometimes get put
in second place

00:04:49.262 --> 00:04:51.220
because the other things
have been prioritized.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.850
And the last one,
operational insight--

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.240
this is operationalized
insight, I should say.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.750
I knew it was going to
be a tongue twister.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.390
This is really where
everyone wants to be.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.850
This is the place where,
essentially, you've

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:07.980
done all this analysis
work, and you're

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:09.990
using it to predict the future.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.980
So you're using it to work
out, for example here, I'm

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.695
trying to work out
how much fuel I need

00:05:15.695 --> 00:05:17.820
or how much I should purchase
for the next quarter.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.480
I'm using these numbers to
actually drive those decisions.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.400
So this is a decision
making space.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.830
Again, some of the
challenges in this area--

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.510
predictive capabilities,
like, what kind

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:28.884
of tools, what
kind of frameworks

00:05:28.884 --> 00:05:32.790
do you want to support,
diverse data sources, again,

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.390
data coming from all
different places.

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.640
Some of it can be stored
easily in the data warehouse.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.490
Some of it's in the
data lake or may

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.790
be not even
available, and again,

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:45.380
having enough capacity
to run this stuff.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:48.740
So I'm going to make some
very gross generalizations.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.356
But what we see
when we're working

00:05:51.356 --> 00:05:52.730
with customers--
and again, these

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:55.690
are generalizations, so they
might not be specific to you.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.840
But some of the stuff
that we see, for example,

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.470
is that a lot of
work is being done.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.870
And a lot of people start from
the operational reporting side.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:07.892
And they want to move to
that operationalized insight.

00:06:07.892 --> 00:06:09.350
But there's a lot
of stuff that has

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:10.910
to be done to get them there.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.400
And they don't want to
sacrifice all the work

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.430
that they've done to have their
operational reporting in place.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:20.020
So if we're going to
migrate the data warehouse,

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.560
we can say, oh, great,
now we can do this--

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.650
we can run some machine
learning models.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.510
And we can take that output
and we can make it available

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.810
so we can do some kind
of recommendation system,

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:30.980
for example.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:34.910
But if they can't support
the reports and the dashboard

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:37.264
that they've been relying
on, then they're like, well,

00:06:37.264 --> 00:06:38.430
first we need to solve that.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:42.460
That's what we see
coming time and again.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.780
And we see often a
lot of customers,

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.570
when they're working with
their stuff on premise,

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:49.490
they struggle for resources.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:52.130
There's a finite number of
resources that they have.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.265
And those resources are--

00:06:54.265 --> 00:06:56.859
there's a lot of claim
on those resources.

00:06:56.859 --> 00:06:59.150
So there's a good reason for
them to move to the cloud,

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:01.620
because we can give them more
and more resources to use.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.100
We don't have those
same constraints.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.850
But yeah, hopefully that gives
some indication the reasons

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.330
why we would do this.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.630
So as another kind
of generalization,

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.130
this is the kind
of movement we see.

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:15.213
We see people starting
with operational reporting,

00:07:15.213 --> 00:07:18.380
with ad hoc reporting,
and moving up

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.250
into that
operationalized insight.

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.470
And of course, a lot of
that operationalized insight

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.092
is based off that ad
hoc analysis as well,

00:07:26.092 --> 00:07:27.550
building out those
machine learning

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.316
models or other prediction
models to actually take you

00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:31.690
to that place
where you can start

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:33.830
putting this into
some kind of process

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:35.270
to actually make decisions.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:42.820
So obviously, no slide deck
is complete without Maslow's

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:43.720
pyramid.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:47.500
So here's a best attempt
at something very colorful.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:50.590
What we've basically got here
is, if you look at each level,

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:52.960
to get to that top piece,
this is the kind of stuff

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.140
that is built on.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.245
So we've got data quality,
data timing that's

00:07:56.245 --> 00:07:57.580
right there at the bottom.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.770
We've got the capacity
and the tooling.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.510
You need all of
these things to be

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:04.460
able to fulfill your
different use cases.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:08.990
So this is what your
platform should be providing.

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:09.490
OK.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.640
So why do people--

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.110
well, why do folks actually
consider migrating their data

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:16.032
warehouse in the first place?

00:08:16.032 --> 00:08:18.240
Well, obviously, I've just
been through some of them,

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.290
but just to kind of recap
cost of infrastructure,

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:25.660
not having enough resources.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.810
Another one that we see is
there are specific skill sets

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:31.180
that you need to run
some of these systems.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:35.094
So for example, let's say
you're running Hive on Hadoop.

00:08:35.094 --> 00:08:36.510
You might have a
whole team that's

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:40.890
actually required just to
support that Hive installation.

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:43.299
And that might not be
core to their business.

00:08:43.299 --> 00:08:44.850
So you're thinking,
hang on, I don't

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.975
mind having one or two
people doing this but having

00:08:46.975 --> 00:08:47.580
a whole team.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:49.780
I could have that team
doing something else.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:52.290
So these are things
to think about.

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:55.920
And some of the stuff
that kind of limits

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.040
there as well are
things like upgrades.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.560
So I'm sure some of you went
to the Dataproc talk yesterday.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:03.780
And you would have
seen, for example,

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:05.610
it's very easy in
the cloud to run

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.390
different versions of Hadoop,
whereas when you are on premise

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:12.930
and you need to update because
you're missing some capability,

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:14.850
that might mean shutting
off the cluster,

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:17.640
actually performing the update,
and if it doesn't go well

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:18.300
rolling back.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.490
So you have this
game that you have

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:23.760
to play to actually give that
capability that you're trying

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.650
to offer to your end users.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.750
And concurrency often
suffers as well.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:30.630
A lot of these systems
have the performance

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.610
that you require to
actually run the query,

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.340
but when it comes to many
users asking questions

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:40.960
at the same time, there is some
trade off in concurrency there.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.080
So Google Cloud's
data warehouse.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:44.879
I'm just going to
take a sip of water.

00:09:44.879 --> 00:09:46.170
That's always good for YouTube.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.410
So Google Cloud's
data warehouse.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:54.010
Well, BigQuery, as
you know, that's

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:57.276
why you're here, obviously.

00:09:57.276 --> 00:09:58.650
I probably don't
need to tell you

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.510
any of these things, because
you've probably already

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:01.950
been looking at BigQuery
and you're thinking,

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:02.920
yeah, that's great.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:03.990
I know BigQuery.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:06.430
But just for anyone
that hasn't seen it,

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:08.950
these are some of the
things that we call out

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.590
on BigQuery for the reasons why
customers are going to move.

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:13.590
Later I'll look at some
of the new features that

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.130
have been mentioned today.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:16.740
But some of the
important ones here,

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.500
and I think the most crucial--
when you're migrating a data

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.950
warehouse, it's like can
I use the skills I already

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:24.410
have, so the standard SQL.

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:26.310
That's a hugely important one.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.535
Can it cope with the amount
of data and the concurrency

00:10:29.535 --> 00:10:33.179
that I need to actually get
the benefits that everyone's

00:10:33.179 --> 00:10:34.470
able to perform their analysis?

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.200
I can run all these
different types of reports.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.920
And you know, security
here, so the encryption--

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:43.690
we have default encryption
on all of our storage.

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:46.150
You can choose to bring
your own encryption keys.

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:48.450
So for particularly
sensitive workloads,

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:50.730
managing your own
encryption keys

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.910
might be a good thing
for you to be able to do.

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:55.650
That's particularly important
in something like the finance

00:10:55.650 --> 00:11:00.320
sector with certain data, and
then highly available, managed.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:02.820
You don't have to focus on
the operation of BigQuery.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.210
You can actually focus on using
BigQuery and actually creating

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:08.980
your use cases.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:10.660
So very high level
architecture--

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:13.070
this is what
BigQuery looks like.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:16.960
There are a number of parts, but
I will try and break it down.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.270
So storage is
separated from compute.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.850
This is common across
all of Google products.

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:24.370
We'll see it a little
bit more in a moment.

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:26.710
That petabyte network
in between-- it's

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:28.500
actually 1.3 petabytes.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:31.570
That's something like
130,000 machines talking

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:35.170
at 10 gigabits per second.

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:37.060
What that essentially
means is that we

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:41.710
can have our compute
disaggregated from our storage.

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:44.420
We can grow either
one independently.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:49.730
We can serve multiple queries
from the same storage.

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:53.710
For example, if you were
trying to create your own data

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:58.492
warehouse using Hive and
Parquet and Hadoop, for example,

00:11:58.492 --> 00:11:59.950
you would have to
make some choices

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:01.390
as you grow that cluster.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:03.880
There is a ratio
of CPU and memory

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:07.930
that you attach to those disks
that you're making available.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:09.625
And obviously you can optimize.

00:12:09.625 --> 00:12:13.494
You can say I'm going to have
bigger disks and fewer CPUs,

00:12:13.494 --> 00:12:16.160
or the other way around, but you
still need to think about that.

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.260
They're not separate.

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:18.130
Here they are.

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:19.630
And that's really
possible because

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:22.679
of the network in between
and how fast that is.

00:12:22.679 --> 00:12:24.220
Other things which
are very important

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:29.045
here is that we have, on the
right-hand side, standard SQL,

00:12:29.045 --> 00:12:31.780
so everyone can use a language
which is common to them.

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:33.400
And on the left hand
side, we support

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.320
both batch and streaming data.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.317
So you can have a table
with like a petabyte of data

00:12:38.317 --> 00:12:40.150
in that table and you
can still be streaming

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:41.470
in the latest updates.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:44.770
And you can query over both
of those in the same query.

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:47.920
So you can have a query which
is basically running and giving

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.740
you the context between
what's happening now

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:56.880
and the historical data
from running a SQL query.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.715
So this is not unique to
BigQuery, that separation.

00:12:59.715 --> 00:13:01.674
This is across all
of our products.

00:13:01.674 --> 00:13:03.090
So if you look at
the bottom here,

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:05.776
these are all the
different types of storage.

00:13:05.776 --> 00:13:07.400
I have a storage
session this afternoon

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.130
if you want to know
more about that.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:11.320
But the different
types of storage--

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:12.500
I'll call a few out here.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:16.250
So BigQuery is columnar storage.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:18.930
So Dremel is the
original white paper.

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.075
We now call it Capacitor.

00:13:20.075 --> 00:13:22.560
It's like an
evolution from there.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:26.460
To give you an idea,
storage formats like Parquet

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:28.190
are actually based
off of Dremel.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:34.310
So Bigtable-- it's key
value is column family.

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:36.890
It's really good for
random reads and writes.

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:39.740
Sometimes you might want
to query that with SQL.

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:43.400
Well, that's possible through
BigQuery doing federation.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.470
You can also-- let's
say you had a data lake

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:48.320
and you've got Avro files,
CSV files, ORC files,

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:51.350
Parquet files,
JSON files, and you

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:54.830
don't want to load those yet
into BigQuery for some reason.

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:57.410
You can have them available
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:00.450
And you can run federated
queries over them as well.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:02.810
So some of them you can do
federating queries and others

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:04.110
you have to load for a job.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:07.490
But CSV, Avro, and JSON all
support the federated query

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:08.384
model.

00:14:08.384 --> 00:14:10.550
So you have different options
available to you here.

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:13.580
I mean, obviously if you want to
get the best native performance

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:15.890
of BigQuery, then
the columnar storage

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:20.690
is obviously suited for
running analytical queries.

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:23.510
That's why that storage exists.

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:24.710
But this gives you an idea.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:27.410
And the same data that
you're storing in BigQuery

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:30.410
maybe you need to make available
to your machine learning

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:32.100
algorithms for training.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.730
So you'd have at the top
there Cloud ML Engine, which

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:38.280
can access BigQuery and actually
pull from that data storage

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:40.580
to actually go and
train this model.

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:42.830
So it's really about thinking
about which storage fits

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:44.270
and what type of
processing fits.

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:47.450
So on the top, if it's
BigQuery, it's SQL.

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:50.690
On the bottom, if it's
BigQuery, it's columnar storage.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:53.630
On the bottom, if you have a
random read/write use case,

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:55.760
you would think about
something like Bigtable.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:58.085
If you wanted to run some
federated ad hoc queries

00:14:58.085 --> 00:15:00.020
with that, you
could use BigQuery.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.380
If you wanted to have
the columnar performance,

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.130
then you would use
something like Dataflow

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:05.950
to take that BigQuery data--

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:07.914
that Bigtable data, sorry--

00:15:07.914 --> 00:15:09.830
and load it into BigQuery
so you get a benefit

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:11.220
from the high performance.

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:13.060
And if you need both
types of access,

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:15.320
so the high
analytical performance

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:18.180
and the random read/write
stuff, use two types of storage.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.060
Just make sure the
pipelines feeding

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:22.580
that are idempotent
functional so

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:26.810
that you can reload it and
have a consistent output.

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:30.890
We will look a little bit about
that in the demo in a moment.

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:35.264
So moving on here, this
is one of our customers.

00:15:35.264 --> 00:15:36.680
I'll let you read
this yourselves.

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:40.850
But this is basically-- well,
let me bring up the bigger one.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:42.290
The reason for
doing this together

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.940
with us is that we work
on providing a system

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.516
managed service and you
work on your use cases.

00:15:47.516 --> 00:15:48.890
The more we can
do that, the more

00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:51.410
we can have a successful
partnership together.

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:55.130
But we have our engineering
focus on bringing capabilities,

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:56.720
and you have your
engineering focused

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:00.159
on using those capabilities
to do cool stuff with it.

00:16:00.159 --> 00:16:02.450
So I'm going to move very
quickly through the reference

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.910
architectures.

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:06.460
This first reference
architecture--

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:07.730
let me get back a slide.

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:10.250
This is like, OK, I'm going
to do a migration now.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:11.540
Where do I start?

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:13.266
So obviously you
don't jump and say,

00:16:13.266 --> 00:16:15.140
click your fingers and
the migration is done.

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:16.145
Migrations are complex.

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:22.370
This is the first set up
that we'd like to advocate.

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:25.760
So on the left hand side, we've
basically got your current

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:27.750
on prem situation.

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:29.930
This may look like what
you're running on prem.

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:30.800
It may not.

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.510
But to give you
some idea, this is

00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:34.700
kind of like-- you have
your source systems,

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:36.800
you have some kind
of ETL process

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:39.050
which loads that
data into staging,

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:43.070
runs, does some transmitter
maybe denormalization,

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:45.320
dimensionalizes
that data, makes it

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:46.987
available in a data warehouse.

00:16:46.987 --> 00:16:49.070
And there's a whole bunch
of reports and use cases

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:51.500
that are taking advantage
of that data there.

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:54.020
And the orchestration piece
in the middle that's basically

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:55.811
allowing you to do all
of that, making sure

00:16:55.811 --> 00:16:57.590
that happens every
day, making sure things

00:16:57.590 --> 00:16:59.229
happen in the right order.

00:16:59.229 --> 00:17:00.770
So in the first move
that we're going

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:04.200
to look at in the demo at the
moment is actually a mirroring.

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:06.980
So we will read from the
current data warehouse.

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:08.770
And our goal will be
in the first instance

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:12.609
is to have the same
available in BigQuery.

00:17:12.609 --> 00:17:14.480
Once that's available
in BigQuery,

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:17.119
you can start denormalizing
some of that data,

00:17:17.119 --> 00:17:18.950
aggregating some
of the data to make

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:21.300
it available for use cases.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:23.510
So for new reporting use cases.

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:25.040
We're going to use
data studio here,

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:28.550
but there's a whole bunch of
tools out there from partners

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:31.255
that you can use for the
reporting as for the ETL

00:17:31.255 --> 00:17:33.576
as well, but we'll come
onto that in a moment.

00:17:33.576 --> 00:17:35.450
So the piece that we're
going to look at here

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:37.970
is what do we need to do to
be able to mirror the data

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:40.700
warehouse onto BigQuery.

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:42.750
And to recap why
are we doing this,

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:44.390
we want to get results fast.

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:47.090
We want to have the least
amount of risk to our business.

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:48.990
We're selling them
on quite a big thing.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.470
So we don't want to say,
look, go ahead and do this,

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:56.210
and then once you've done it,
we will see how long it takes.

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:56.710
No.

00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:59.710
Let's take a route where
we can do some of this,

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:01.660
show them how good
it works for them,

00:18:01.660 --> 00:18:03.090
show them what
they get out of it.

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:05.960
You know, don't put their
current processing at risk.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:08.960
And once that works, then we
can move onto the next phase.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:10.300
And we also get GCP adoption.

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:11.800
You know, there are
many people that

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:13.960
haven't used all
the GCP tools, maybe

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:16.120
in your business, other
developers, other analysts,

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:17.440
other business users.

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:19.280
And they get from
this the opportunity

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:21.160
to start working
with those tools.

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:23.360
And importantly, we
can benchmark stuff.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:24.270
Numbers talk.

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:27.634
So we can actually show to
any of our stakeholders, look,

00:18:27.634 --> 00:18:29.800
you know, we were running
these queries here before.

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:30.850
It was taking this much.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:32.479
We had this kind of concurrency.

00:18:32.479 --> 00:18:34.270
We could only support
this number of users.

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:35.860
And now we can do this.

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:39.340
So you get that from
this first architecture.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:40.570
What's the target?

00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:43.600
Well, we don't want to go from
running one data warehouse

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:45.170
to running two data warehouses.

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:47.540
The whole idea is to
do this migration.

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:51.910
So the target is obviously to
actually have the full data

00:18:51.910 --> 00:18:56.220
warehouse process running on GCP
and reading from those sources.

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:58.120
Now those sources
might be on GCP.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:18:59.740
They might be on prem.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:01.480
They could be on
another cloud provider.

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:03.396
But essentially for this
particular migration,

00:19:03.396 --> 00:19:05.260
what we're looking at
is taking that data

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:09.550
from wherever those sources may
be and doing the ETL process,

00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:11.962
doing the data warehouse
process on Google Cloud.

00:19:11.962 --> 00:19:13.420
And that's what
we're going to look

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:15.172
at in the demo, so both stages.

00:19:15.172 --> 00:19:16.630
So with that I'm
going to hand over

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:17.880
to Ashok for the demo section.

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:22.890
ASHOK HOLLA: Thanks, Rob.

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:26.620
All right, let's get into it.

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:28.680
So today we'll look
at what it takes

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:32.130
to do the migration to GCP.

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:36.945
And in this first stage, we look
at mirroring the data warehouse

00:19:36.945 --> 00:19:39.030
to BigQuery.

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:41.730
And we'll start with
the data already landed

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:43.440
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:45.870
So I'll be doing a lot of
back and forth switching

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:48.782
because of the slides and demo.

00:19:48.782 --> 00:19:49.810
OK.

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:53.160
So what we've done is we
are mirroring on prem data

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:55.660
warehouse, right, so we already
have the facts and dimension

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:56.420
data.

00:19:56.420 --> 00:20:01.060
And since facts are
usually larger in size,

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:04.250
we have to organize
them by date.

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:07.690
And let's take a look
at the fact data here.

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:09.250
So they are organized by date.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:12.600
And if something goes
wrong or you update your

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:16.660
on prem data warehouse,
all you do is just reload

00:20:16.660 --> 00:20:17.780
the partition, right?

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:19.690
And in BigQuery we
are going to store

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:22.570
this as a partitioned table.

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:24.460
And dimensions
are usually small.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:26.930
So you don't really
have to partition them.

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:29.770
And it's OK to basically
refresh all the dimension

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:31.150
data every day.

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:35.650
And this also ensures that
any slowly changing dimension

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:38.620
techniques that you have
implied would just carry over

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:40.670
to the data warehouse as well.

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:44.200
And this is how the dimension
data looks for the demo.

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:46.870
And for the demo I'm
using TPC-DS data just

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:50.960
to get a data warehouse set up.

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:54.310
So one second.

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:57.270
I need to get a hang
of the switching here.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:57.850
OK.

00:20:57.850 --> 00:21:00.440
So we looked at the data sets.

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:01.990
So what we are doing
is we are going

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:06.310
to stage the data in Google
Cloud Storage, facts organized

00:21:06.310 --> 00:21:09.780
by date and dimensions
in just a folder.

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:11.890
And we are going to do
a refresh every day.

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:15.130
And then we are going to use
Cloud Composer to basically

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:18.700
orchestrate this workflow
and materialize the table

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:20.590
in the BigQuery data format.

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:22.840
Once that is done,
what we'll do is

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:26.250
we'll run some downstream
aggregation queries.

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:28.040
We can materialise that as well.

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:30.910
And it's always a good practice
to materialize any aggregation

00:21:30.910 --> 00:21:34.850
queries that are frequently used
so that your reports are not

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:36.970
lagging due to
runtime calculations

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:39.690
and also the data is not
open for interpretation.

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:44.480
All right, so let's
take a look at that.

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:45.159
OK.

00:21:45.159 --> 00:21:46.950
So what we are trying
to do is we're trying

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:49.130
to get to this report, right?

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:51.870
So this report is
basically calculating

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:55.770
cumulative sales and
returns, both sales channel.

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:59.600
And below we are seeing a
scatterplot of net sales

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:00.790
by different channels.

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:02.520
So how do we get there?

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:05.400
So as I said, we are using
Cloud Composer to basically

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:07.590
orchestrate this workflow.

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:10.840
So what we are seeing
here is, on the left,

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:12.460
we are creating staging tables.

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:15.230
So we are basically creating a
federated BigQuery table which

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:18.630
is pointing to a file
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:22.830
and then we materialized that
into a native BigQuery table.

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:25.935
And once that is done, we do
the same thing with fact tables.

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:31.480
But it may so happen
that I know you probably

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:34.540
have to replay a
partition because you've

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:35.860
updated something on prem.

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:39.066
So you have to replay the
partition for your fact data.

00:22:39.066 --> 00:22:40.440
So what we have
done is basically

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:43.660
we have added a Boolean
variable in the airflow code

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:46.970
to basically just load fact
data and not dimension data.

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:50.150
So in this way you have a
repeatable idempotent process

00:22:50.150 --> 00:22:53.330
for loading fact data.

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:56.520
And this is a code
that does just that.

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:57.020
Right.

00:22:57.020 --> 00:23:00.250
So all I'm doing is this is
an airflow variable which

00:23:00.250 --> 00:23:02.480
I have defined up here.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:05.690
And then if it is yes, then
we load dimension and staging.

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:07.525
If it is no, we just
lost the dimension.

00:23:07.525 --> 00:23:08.025
All right.

00:23:08.025 --> 00:23:10.250
So let me just trigger
this and then I'll

00:23:10.250 --> 00:23:13.519
walk you through the
individual processes.

00:23:13.519 --> 00:23:15.060
So I'm going to
trigger it for today.

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:18.150
So let's just quickly check if
the variable is set for today.

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.370
It is yes, 25.

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:23.554
So I'm going to go back
and trigger the table--

00:23:23.554 --> 00:23:24.220
trigger the DAG.

00:23:27.731 --> 00:23:28.230
All right.

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:29.530
So it is running.

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:33.470
So let's just go back there.

00:23:33.470 --> 00:23:34.030
OK.

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:36.820
So I spoke about
external tables being

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:39.640
used for staging purpose
and Google Cloud Storage

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:40.960
being the staging area.

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:44.620
Let's take a look at
the definition of those.

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:49.210
So the dimensions table, as
I said, is refreshed daily.

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:51.670
So it's just pointing to
a location in Google Cloud

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:55.420
Storage without any
database partitions.

00:23:55.420 --> 00:23:59.560
And the fact tables are
partitioned by date,

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:03.340
hence they are stored
in a date folder.

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:06.080
So it is today, right?

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:09.460
And for materializing those
tables from the staging area

00:24:09.460 --> 00:24:11.050
into BigQuery native,
all we are doing

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:13.939
is just a simple
select star a table.

00:24:13.939 --> 00:24:15.730
Well, then you could
obviously get creative

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:17.620
and then add your
own transformations

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:20.470
or do further
denormalization if needed.

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:25.020
The aggregation
query that I'm using

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:28.470
is a simple query that
aggregates all the sales

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:32.500
and returns by three different
channels, their sales--

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:35.430
the store, web, and
catalog channels.

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:38.640
And I'm just doing a
cumulative sales and returns.

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:41.010
Just a summation on all
the three up to today.

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:49.580
Let's take a look at the
DAG if it's finished.

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:50.730
That's still running.

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:51.688
We'll give it a minute.

00:24:56.510 --> 00:24:57.460
OK.

00:24:57.460 --> 00:25:01.440
So as you can see, the staging
areas have been created.

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:03.096
And then they have
been materialized.

00:25:03.096 --> 00:25:04.470
Now we are at a
stage where we're

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:06.780
trying to materialize fact.

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:08.080
That is done as well.

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:08.847
So it's success.

00:25:08.847 --> 00:25:10.930
Now it's the last stage
where we are materializing

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:12.970
the aggregation query as well.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:16.350
So once this is done,
we should be able to--

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:18.450
we should be able to see
a partition in the sales

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:21.450
by channel table, which I
created for materializing

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:22.560
the ideation query.

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:24.280
And this is done as well.

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:26.580
Let's take a look
at the partition.

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:28.930
So I'm querying the
partition summary metadata

00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:30.790
for that particular table.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:34.570
And if I run this query, I
should see a partition here.

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:35.740
So as you can see--

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:38.320
so 425, which was
created just now.

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:40.590
Now, if I go to the
report, I should

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:45.440
be able to see the report
being updated for today.

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:46.660
There you go.

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:50.710
So that's demo one
where we mirrored the

00:25:50.710 --> 00:25:53.320
on prem data warehouse
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:55.890
Now let's take a look--

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:57.075
I need to switch.

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:01.890
Oops.

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:03.002
Sorry.

00:26:03.002 --> 00:26:03.710
Sorry about that.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:08.580
OK.

00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:11.820
So let's take a look
at demo two wherein we

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:13.210
have done the cut off, right?

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:16.000
So the end state is we
are running the data

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:18.470
warehouse in Google
Cloud Storage

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:22.617
and what does it take
to make that functional.

00:26:25.299 --> 00:26:26.200
OK.

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:30.449
So this workflow looks quite
similar to what we had earlier.

00:26:30.449 --> 00:26:31.990
But there's a
significant difference.

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:36.370
We are partitioning
dimensions as well.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:38.122
Now it's obvious why
we partition facts,

00:26:38.122 --> 00:26:39.080
because they're larger.

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:41.010
And I've spoken about that.

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:42.500
But why dimensions?

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:45.510
Number one, to keep the whole
workflow simple and easy

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:46.740
to maintain.

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:50.266
And we have a reproducible
and idempotent process as far

00:26:50.266 --> 00:26:52.050
as data pipeline is concerned.

00:26:52.050 --> 00:26:56.132
And also because NCDs
are cumbersome, right?

00:26:56.132 --> 00:26:57.840
Slowly changing
dimensions are cumbersome

00:26:57.840 --> 00:26:59.770
and can get easily complicated.

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:01.560
With this, what you
can do is you're

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:07.650
just doing a refresh off
your everyday dimension data

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:09.480
into a new partition
in BigQuery.

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:12.130
This gives you
bitemporality, another easy

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:13.830
to implement bitemporality.

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:17.919
You can go back in time and
look at the state of your data

00:27:17.919 --> 00:27:20.460
at that point in time by just
querying a particular partition

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:21.240
and dimension.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.720
So you don't have to
create complex joins,

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:26.520
or you don't have to have
complex ETL processes

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:27.910
to maintain.

00:27:27.910 --> 00:27:30.030
So that's why we are
partitioning dimension data

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:30.700
as well.

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:32.310
So this is part of the
functional data engineering

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:33.726
approach which we
have taken here.

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:40.040
The fact data is
partitioned by event date,

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:43.070
and the dimensioned data is
partitioned by the processing

00:27:43.070 --> 00:27:45.582
date when you processed this.

00:27:45.582 --> 00:27:46.390
OK.

00:27:46.390 --> 00:27:51.880
So I'm going to switch back
here and go to my second demo.

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:57.520
So I said that dimension and
fact are both partitioned

00:27:57.520 --> 00:27:58.760
once you do a cut off.

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:00.550
Let's take a look at
the dimension data.

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:03.170
So it is, in fact,
partitioned by date.

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:05.800
Let's take a look
at the DAG that

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:08.170
supports this whole workflow.

00:28:08.170 --> 00:28:11.170
So it's similar to the
one that we had earlier.

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:15.050
We are integrating
staging tables,

00:28:15.050 --> 00:28:17.840
integrating a federated table
in BigQuery for both domination

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:18.940
and fact.

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:21.370
We are materializing
dimension data.

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:25.000
So note that we are
using TPC-E certification

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:27.820
data, which is in normal form.

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:31.610
So obviously we have to create
facts and dimensioned data when

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:33.100
we materialize in BigQuery.

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:35.620
So this task is doing just that.

00:28:35.620 --> 00:28:37.780
It is materializing
the dimensioned data.

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:41.590
Once that is done, we are going
to materialize the fact data.

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:43.360
And that will be the
end of this workflow.

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:45.010
So let me just trigger
this and then walk you

00:28:45.010 --> 00:28:46.093
through the whole process.

00:28:53.530 --> 00:28:55.450
OK.

00:28:55.450 --> 00:28:56.760
So that's triggered.

00:28:56.760 --> 00:29:00.150
Now let's take a look
at the various queries.

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:04.440
So I said that the dimension
and fact tables are both

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:05.700
organized by date.

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:07.530
So this is a fact table thread.

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:09.329
It's organized by date.

00:29:09.329 --> 00:29:11.370
And let's take a look at
the company table, which

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:12.570
is the dimensioned data.

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:15.500
It's also organized by
date in the staging area.

00:29:15.500 --> 00:29:17.580
And the queries that
I'm using to materialize

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:20.490
these tables into BigQuery
are simple joints.

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:22.890
So basically I'm
denormalizing the data.

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:24.810
This is for trade.

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:27.720
And I'm basically joining that
with status type and trade

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:28.500
type.

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:30.570
And for company, I'm
basically joining that

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:33.450
with status type and address.

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:36.660
So you could actually
use any ETL tool

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:38.620
of your choice to do this.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:42.570
But in this case, I'm just
using SQL and the staging area

00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:44.760
being Google Cloud storage.

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:48.750
But one thing to note is
that for a company, which

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:51.570
is a dimension
table, we are having

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:54.389
a partition date, which is
called the create a date here.

00:29:54.389 --> 00:29:56.430
And you're partitioning
based on that date, which

00:29:56.430 --> 00:29:59.140
is the day when you
run the workflow.

00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:02.850
And for trade, which
is a fact data,

00:30:02.850 --> 00:30:05.880
we are basically partitioning
by the event date, which

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:09.670
is [INAUDIBLE] in this example.

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:11.920
Let's also take a look
at one of the queries

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:15.160
that we'll be running
after the DAG completes.

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:18.220
So in the previous example,
we saw how we could create

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:20.650
and how we could
materialize a query

00:30:20.650 --> 00:30:22.270
and then create a
report on top of it.

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:24.850
But BigQuery can also be
used for ad hoc analysis.

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:27.170
And this query is
basically calculating

00:30:27.170 --> 00:30:32.260
the 60-second simple moving
average on bid prices

00:30:32.260 --> 00:30:35.770
in a three-hour period
for all the securities

00:30:35.770 --> 00:30:39.890
that just get loaded
once the DAG completes.

00:30:39.890 --> 00:30:42.200
Let's take a look at the
DAG if it is completed.

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:46.480
It's running.

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:47.390
It'll be done soon.

00:30:51.746 --> 00:30:52.720
OK.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:56.540
So the materialization of
dimensions is complete.

00:30:56.540 --> 00:31:00.160
Now the next stage is basically
materialization of the fact

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:01.374
table, which is straight.

00:31:01.374 --> 00:31:03.790
And once that is done, we'll
run our moving average query.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.040
It's running.

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:16.120
It'll happen.

00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:18.327
So what I'm doing here
is I'm deleting-- so

00:31:18.327 --> 00:31:20.410
basically, we are creating
a staging table, right?

00:31:20.410 --> 00:31:22.810
So the files are in
Google Cloud Storage

00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:24.630
and they are creating
a federated table.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:26.590
So obviously every
day when you run,

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:30.815
you have to point that table to
the right in a file in Google

00:31:30.815 --> 00:31:31.720
Cloud Storage.

00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:34.480
So hence the first step is
to basically delete the table

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:38.050
definition and then recreate
it with the file for that date.

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:40.480
So that is what this stage does.

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:43.900
So now the create
stage table is done.

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:44.896
It's a success.

00:31:44.896 --> 00:31:46.270
Now, finally, what
we're doing is

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:48.630
we are going to run the
query that I showed earlier,

00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:51.910
the denormalization query which
joins trade table with address

00:31:51.910 --> 00:31:56.035
and status type and then creates
the fact table in BigQuery.

00:31:56.035 --> 00:31:57.701
It should be done in
a few seconds here.

00:32:01.390 --> 00:32:02.610
And that's done.

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:07.170
So let's go and
run our query here

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:10.410
for today, which is a moving
average, a 60-second moving

00:32:10.410 --> 00:32:13.980
average over a three-hour
period for all the securities.

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:14.970
And there you go.

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:16.710
So it just ran.

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:18.880
And we have all
the moving average.

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:21.790
So BigQuery can be used for
ad hoc analysis as well.

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:25.630
And that completes the end to
end workflow, so for when you--

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:28.110
when you do a complete cut off.

00:32:28.110 --> 00:32:30.990
Now let's look at another
interesting concept

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:33.727
in BigQuery.

00:32:33.727 --> 00:32:36.060
So far we've seen a functional
data engineering approach

00:32:36.060 --> 00:32:40.740
where we create a repeatable
and idempotent processes.

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:43.960
And we basically
replay the workflow

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:45.450
and then update the partitions.

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:45.990
Right?

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:48.060
So we don't do any
DML statements.

00:32:48.060 --> 00:32:51.960
But DMLs can be friendly
when used in the right format

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:53.340
and the right way.

00:32:53.340 --> 00:32:56.320
And when you do a
DML update, and you

00:32:56.320 --> 00:32:59.310
update large chunk of data in
BigQuery, but what if something

00:32:59.310 --> 00:32:59.890
goes wrong?

00:32:59.890 --> 00:33:01.360
So how do you recover from it.

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:04.470
So we're going to take a
look at that exactly now.

00:33:04.470 --> 00:33:07.290
So what I'm showing
here is a query

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:10.970
that basically creates the
trade table that we just loaded

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:15.080
and for a particular
customer ID 472.

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:18.670
So let's take a look as to
what the data looks like.

00:33:18.670 --> 00:33:21.390
Now, let's say that I
want to update this trade

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:24.270
table for this
particular customer

00:33:24.270 --> 00:33:25.620
for a particular security.

00:33:25.620 --> 00:33:32.160
But what I did instead is I
updated the commission column

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:35.410
for all the rows for
that particular customer.

00:33:35.410 --> 00:33:37.330
So that's obviously an error.

00:33:37.330 --> 00:33:39.280
So I realized that
I made a mistake.

00:33:39.280 --> 00:33:41.050
So how do I recover from this?

00:33:41.050 --> 00:33:42.780
So there two options, ideally.

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:45.055
The first option
is go back in time.

00:33:45.055 --> 00:33:46.430
All you have to
do is just replay

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:48.780
your workflow with
your partition date

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:50.751
equals 22, in this case.

00:33:50.751 --> 00:33:51.250
Right.

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:52.350
So July 22.

00:33:52.350 --> 00:33:54.060
And then you just
override the partition

00:33:54.060 --> 00:33:56.250
and then move on
from there, or you

00:33:56.250 --> 00:33:58.100
could use BigQuery snapshots.

00:33:58.100 --> 00:33:58.600
Right.

00:33:58.600 --> 00:34:00.930
So BigQuery snapshots
are automatically

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.270
created and maintained
for you for seven days.

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:06.360
And then you can go
back in time and then

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:09.730
get the state of your table
at that point in time.

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:12.719
So we only use BigQuery
snapshots and DML

00:34:12.719 --> 00:34:16.170
to recover from this error.

00:34:16.170 --> 00:34:17.659
But before we do
that, let's take

00:34:17.659 --> 00:34:23.800
a look at what the status of
the table is after the update.

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:26.239
So what I did is I updated
the commission column

00:34:26.239 --> 00:34:29.969
with equals $245
for all the rows

00:34:29.969 --> 00:34:31.230
for that particular customer.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:34.560
So this is how it looks like.

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:36.790
Let's look at the
BigQuery snapshot,

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:42.620
so how it was five minutes
ago before I did my update.

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:44.409
So as you can see,
those securities

00:34:44.409 --> 00:34:47.260
have the right
commission values.

00:34:47.260 --> 00:34:50.830
Now we're going to use that and
create a temporary table, which

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:53.020
we are going to then use
and do an update join

00:34:53.020 --> 00:34:55.840
on the tray table that
we erroneously updated.

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:01.750
So the temporary
table is created.

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:04.460
Now I'm going to
run an update join

00:35:04.460 --> 00:35:08.770
with the temporary table and my
trade table for the partition

00:35:08.770 --> 00:35:11.200
that I just messed up.

00:35:15.024 --> 00:35:15.870
OK.

00:35:15.870 --> 00:35:17.010
So that's done.

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:20.970
So what it did is it basically
updated all my customer

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:24.210
rows for customer equals
472 from my new BigQuery

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:27.690
snapshot, the temporary table
that I just created from it.

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:29.880
Let's take a look
at how the data

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:31.380
looks like after correction.

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:34.590
So it should be similar to
what we looked in the BigQuery

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:36.540
snapshot and it is.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:38.520
It is now at $45.

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:41.940
Now we can just go back
and update for the security

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:43.900
that we really
intended to update.

00:35:51.490 --> 00:35:53.230
That's done.

00:35:53.230 --> 00:35:56.560
And then we'll just see
how it looks like once

00:35:56.560 --> 00:36:01.230
it's updated So all good.

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:03.656
So there are no backlashes.

00:36:03.656 --> 00:36:04.530
Right.

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:06.710
So that's the end of the demo.

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:09.780
Before I hand it
off to Robert, I'm

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:12.270
going to quickly talk
about various ways

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:14.350
to ingest data into GCP.

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:16.987
So we have looked at and are
using Google Cloud Storage

00:36:16.987 --> 00:36:17.820
as the staging area.

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:20.910
So you can use GS Util and
the storage transfer service

00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:22.740
or the data transfer
service that

00:36:22.740 --> 00:36:25.710
comes native to Google to
ingest your data to GCP.

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:28.020
But there are a plethora
of partner products

00:36:28.020 --> 00:36:29.400
as well which you can use.

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:31.420
And you might already
be using these today

00:36:31.420 --> 00:36:34.320
in your existing toolkit.

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:37.320
And it's an easy switch
to basically migrate

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:40.470
data from your on prem
systems or your source systems

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:41.640
into GCP.

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:42.390
Robert, you're up.

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:47.970
ROBERT SAXBY: Thank you, Ashok.

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:51.990
So hopefully that showed you how
easy some of this stuff can be.

00:36:51.990 --> 00:36:53.280
I mean we saw two approaches.

00:36:53.280 --> 00:36:55.320
We saw the functional
engineering approach,

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:57.370
overwriting partitions,
keeping things

00:36:57.370 --> 00:36:59.847
idempotent or repeatable.

00:36:59.847 --> 00:37:01.680
And as Ashok demonstrated
in the last piece,

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:04.710
if you do have DML statements
that you're going to run over

00:37:04.710 --> 00:37:07.140
multiple days over
a very large table,

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:10.020
you have the BigQuery basically
time travel functionality

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:13.150
for the last seven days to
actually go back and restore.

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:16.520
So that will hopefully help
you in some of this work

00:37:16.520 --> 00:37:17.690
that you're going to do.

00:37:17.690 --> 00:37:20.580
And again, BigQuery is--

00:37:20.580 --> 00:37:23.320
it's the crown jewels
of our data products.

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:26.600
And it really sits there in
the middle in a huge ecosystem.

00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:29.810
And these are some of the
partners for the integration.

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:31.290
There's a whole
bunch of partners

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:33.770
as well for reporting
and everything else.

00:37:33.770 --> 00:37:38.010
So it's really depending on
how you want to approach this,

00:37:38.010 --> 00:37:39.490
you're in good hands.

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:41.430
So let me just, for
anyone that missed

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:43.380
any of these things,
some of the new product

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:45.810
features, you saw
throughout this demo today

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:47.250
the new BigQuery UI.

00:37:47.250 --> 00:37:49.060
You can already go and use that.

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:51.870
So for those of you
who are saying, hey,

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:53.400
the BigQuery UI looks different.

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:54.725
What's this new thing up here?

00:37:54.725 --> 00:37:55.600
This is not BigQuery.

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:58.590
That's the new UI.

00:37:58.590 --> 00:38:00.564
This morning you
heard about BQ ML.

00:38:00.564 --> 00:38:01.980
So these are other
things that you

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:06.600
can start to do once you're
operating in BigQuery.

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:08.230
We got partitioning
at the moment.

00:38:08.230 --> 00:38:12.260
So you can do partitioning
on like the insertion date

00:38:12.260 --> 00:38:14.430
or you can choose
your own date column

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:16.590
as we saw here with
two different examples

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:17.740
of partitioning.

00:38:17.740 --> 00:38:19.680
We saw an example
where we were actually

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:21.000
partitioning on a logical date.

00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:24.300
Because for our fact
table, that fact data,

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:26.420
we expect it to be
quite large, and we just

00:38:26.420 --> 00:38:28.890
want to have the
ability to replay

00:38:28.890 --> 00:38:31.650
the events or the
transactions for a given day.

00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:34.710
And in the second use case
with the dimension data,

00:38:34.710 --> 00:38:38.164
even if your dimension
data is millions of rows,

00:38:38.164 --> 00:38:40.080
if you think about that
in context of BigQuery

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:41.940
and these systems
that you can use,

00:38:41.940 --> 00:38:45.349
just replaying that again and
having a new snapshot each day

00:38:45.349 --> 00:38:47.640
really helps you overcome
some of those slowly changing

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:50.531
dimension property so you have
a different-- the partitioning

00:38:50.531 --> 00:38:52.530
we're using there was
basically on a date, which

00:38:52.530 --> 00:38:55.820
is the insertion date.

00:38:55.820 --> 00:38:58.390
Well, available now
is clustering in beta.

00:38:58.390 --> 00:38:59.820
So if you can
imagine clustering,

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:01.440
basically you partition
first on date,

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:03.148
and then you would
have some other column

00:39:03.148 --> 00:39:06.060
with a high cardinality which
you basically cluster on.

00:39:06.060 --> 00:39:08.130
So again, we're reducing
the amount of data

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:11.880
that we need to pick up
in the initial scan, which

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:15.547
is going to make the query
cheaper to run, faster to run.

00:39:15.547 --> 00:39:17.880
So if you have good use cases
for this-- so think about,

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:19.890
first of all, you're
joining or filtering by date

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:22.020
and then you're joining or
filtering by something else.

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:22.936
This would be a good--

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:26.980
yeah.

00:39:26.980 --> 00:39:30.570
How do I say feature to
actually go and explore.

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:33.780
Something we didn't show here,
but Data Studio Explorer.

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:36.129
It's basically from
going from BigQuery

00:39:36.129 --> 00:39:37.920
and clicking and actually
being able to see

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:41.310
the results of that query
directly in Data Studio.

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:43.620
There are deeper
integrations with Sheets,

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:47.460
so just making the whole
experience using your datas

00:39:47.460 --> 00:39:50.820
in BigQuery available
for end users who

00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:52.890
are using Google Sheets.

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:56.070
And later this
afternoon at 3:15,

00:39:56.070 --> 00:39:58.320
if you're not coming to
my storage talk obviously,

00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:01.440
then there's a nice
talk about GSI data

00:40:01.440 --> 00:40:02.540
as well, which is amazing.

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:04.373
Some really great demos
to go and see there.

00:40:04.373 --> 00:40:06.700
I'm really excited about that.

00:40:06.700 --> 00:40:08.325
Actually, I might
just miss my own talk

00:40:08.325 --> 00:40:10.770
and go and watch that one.

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:12.480
So we've gone through
quite a lot today.

00:40:12.480 --> 00:40:14.401
But what I want to
hand us off is--

00:40:14.401 --> 00:40:16.650
so we've gone through some
of the reasons why and what

00:40:16.650 --> 00:40:18.220
we can do with this.

00:40:18.220 --> 00:40:19.954
We've gone through
an approach, how

00:40:19.954 --> 00:40:21.870
we could do that, first
the mirroring and then

00:40:21.870 --> 00:40:24.180
the full process running on GCP.

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:25.860
And this last part
is really kind

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:30.390
of like, OK, this is all great,
but how do I organize myself

00:40:30.390 --> 00:40:33.030
together with Google and
together with partners

00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:35.770
to actually go and
do this migration?

00:40:35.770 --> 00:40:37.830
So we put together
a playbook which

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:43.900
we think would help
illustrate how to do this.

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:46.310
So these are the different
stages of the playbook.

00:40:46.310 --> 00:40:48.930
So first of all, it's
documenting the current state,

00:40:48.930 --> 00:40:52.050
understanding exactly
what it is that you have.

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:54.180
The next thing is
outline the experiment.

00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:57.870
And we'll come on to exactly
why we do an experiment.

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:00.600
Getting resources lined up--

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:02.700
you can't do this by yourself.

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:03.945
I mean, you can.

00:41:03.945 --> 00:41:06.570
It might just be one of you, and
it's just your data warehouse,

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:08.310
but typically there
are many, many people

00:41:08.310 --> 00:41:12.030
involved-- stakeholders,
end users, other teams.

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:14.520
So you know all those
resources need to be lined up.

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:16.965
How do we go about committing
and executing this?

00:41:16.965 --> 00:41:19.890
And importantly, once we've
gone through this cycle,

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:22.290
showing the end users,
getting everyone excited

00:41:22.290 --> 00:41:26.190
about the stuff, and then
repeating it for the next bit.

00:41:26.190 --> 00:41:29.930
I guess you could call it some
kind of agile process, no?

00:41:29.930 --> 00:41:32.870
AUDIENCE: Can you speak more
about how you might expand on--

00:41:32.870 --> 00:41:33.661
ROBERT SAXBY: Yeah.

00:41:33.661 --> 00:41:36.130
I'm going to go through
each of these stages now.

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:38.230
So to document
the current state,

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:39.620
I'll go briefly through this.

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:41.240
Why are we here?

00:41:41.240 --> 00:41:44.470
What are we actually
trying to accomplish?

00:41:44.470 --> 00:41:46.730
We're not just migrating
for the sake of migrating.

00:41:46.730 --> 00:41:48.260
There should be
goals that we have

00:41:48.260 --> 00:41:50.630
and we should be able
to articulate them.

00:41:50.630 --> 00:41:51.770
Where are we coming from?

00:41:51.770 --> 00:41:52.820
Get the white board out.

00:41:52.820 --> 00:41:54.320
Get all the people
that are involved

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:56.579
from the different teams
actually explaining

00:41:56.579 --> 00:41:57.620
where you're coming from.

00:41:57.620 --> 00:41:58.580
Explain it to us.

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:00.650
Explain it to the
partner as well.

00:42:00.650 --> 00:42:02.930
If we understand where
you're coming from,

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:04.910
why you have made
certain decisions,

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:07.430
we can take those learnings
into the migration,

00:42:07.430 --> 00:42:09.320
into the new thing.

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:11.310
If we don't know
about those things,

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:14.300
and we is all of us in
this case, so the people

00:42:14.300 --> 00:42:16.530
that are building this,
the partners, all of us.

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:18.110
If we don't know
about those things,

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:20.740
because someone
forgot to tell us,

00:42:20.740 --> 00:42:22.740
then we're going to go
and discover those later.

00:42:22.740 --> 00:42:26.660
And that will be somewhere down
the line after the migration

00:42:26.660 --> 00:42:28.760
or during the
migration when data

00:42:28.760 --> 00:42:30.270
doesn't look exactly the same.

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:32.210
And as someone will
say, well, actually, I

00:42:32.210 --> 00:42:33.680
decided to do it
like this before.

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:36.110
So it's really important
to understand that stuff.

00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:38.480
And find out-- get feedback
from everyone involved.

00:42:38.480 --> 00:42:40.250
What's already working well?

00:42:40.250 --> 00:42:43.485
Nobody likes having a new
solution put in place.

00:42:43.485 --> 00:42:45.110
And they're saying,
hey, this is great,

00:42:45.110 --> 00:42:46.610
but it works better over here.

00:42:46.610 --> 00:42:48.740
You know, that's not the
place you want to be.

00:42:48.740 --> 00:42:50.720
And what's not working well?

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:52.520
Because this will
also help you already

00:42:52.520 --> 00:42:54.260
start to win hearts
and minds of people.

00:42:54.260 --> 00:42:56.630
You say, well, this piece
isn't really working well.

00:42:56.630 --> 00:42:59.220
So if we maybe
explore some of that,

00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:01.040
we can already get
some people who

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:03.914
are standing around the water
cooler talking about, oh great,

00:43:03.914 --> 00:43:05.330
we've actually
solved this problem

00:43:05.330 --> 00:43:07.460
that we've had for a long time.

00:43:07.460 --> 00:43:10.190
So outlining experiment.

00:43:10.190 --> 00:43:13.490
This is really digging into
that piece of the documentation

00:43:13.490 --> 00:43:15.210
and trying to
understand what it is.

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:17.140
What's the one piece that
we're going to take from--

00:43:17.140 --> 00:43:18.620
let's say from all
the possible things

00:43:18.620 --> 00:43:20.661
that we're going to go
and do for this first one,

00:43:20.661 --> 00:43:22.790
this next one, what is
it we're trying to do?

00:43:22.790 --> 00:43:25.970
So what's possible, what
are the constraints, what

00:43:25.970 --> 00:43:27.260
is the value to the business?

00:43:29.810 --> 00:43:33.200
In the initial use cases, as
I said this is an iteration,

00:43:33.200 --> 00:43:36.650
but in those initial use cases,
think about smaller things.

00:43:36.650 --> 00:43:37.834
Get some experience.

00:43:37.834 --> 00:43:40.250
I know I just mentioned that
there's this problem that you

00:43:40.250 --> 00:43:43.370
want to solve, but if you
can already get a quick win

00:43:43.370 --> 00:43:45.770
and just get everyone's hands
accustomed to what you're

00:43:45.770 --> 00:43:48.270
doing, that next
bigger problem's

00:43:48.270 --> 00:43:50.180
going to already
seem a lot easier.

00:43:52.997 --> 00:43:55.580
There are things that you should
be considering along the way.

00:43:55.580 --> 00:43:56.996
You should be
asking us questions.

00:43:56.996 --> 00:44:02.390
An example-- I once
got handed a project

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:05.960
which was all of
the ad data, and it

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:09.560
was a lot for the company I was
working with, being imported

00:44:09.560 --> 00:44:11.389
on premise because
that's how they did it.

00:44:11.389 --> 00:44:13.430
They didn't have the
processing power on premise,

00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:15.770
so they uploaded
it into the cloud.

00:44:15.770 --> 00:44:18.424
They ran a Hadoop
job on that data.

00:44:18.424 --> 00:44:20.090
They then poured it
back and they put it

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:24.090
into the data warehouse, who
at that time was SQL Server.

00:44:24.090 --> 00:44:25.364
We were migrating to BigQuery.

00:44:25.364 --> 00:44:26.280
We had looked at this.

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:27.900
And we said, hang
on a second, do we

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:29.940
need to set up this ETL process?

00:44:29.940 --> 00:44:32.100
And there was a data
transfer service available,

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:34.020
which put the data
straight into BigQuery.

00:44:34.020 --> 00:44:36.750
Now, that's great if you're
working with [INAUDIBLE] GA

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:38.960
data or ad data, which is
already available for you

00:44:38.960 --> 00:44:40.590
in that way.

00:44:40.590 --> 00:44:41.940
That data might not be there.

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:43.470
But there are also, as
we mentioned before,

00:44:43.470 --> 00:44:45.520
partners that are working
on this data as well.

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:48.540
So if you've got sources
which other people are using,

00:44:48.540 --> 00:44:51.760
so our market data, Salesforce
data, all these things,

00:44:51.760 --> 00:44:55.830
think about is are there already
solutions out there for that?

00:44:55.830 --> 00:44:58.680
That's just keeping
everyone alert,

00:44:58.680 --> 00:45:01.100
making sure that we
do useful things.

00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:04.185
Define the time frame,
the requirements,

00:45:04.185 --> 00:45:06.630
all this kind of stuff, and
sketch out an architecture

00:45:06.630 --> 00:45:07.440
for the experiment.

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:10.020
And try to make sure
that everyone is aligned.

00:45:10.020 --> 00:45:12.120
I realize some of this
really speaks to itself,

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:14.520
but sometimes just going
through it with everyone

00:45:14.520 --> 00:45:18.900
is really useful in
getting everyone aligned.

00:45:18.900 --> 00:45:20.610
Lining up the
resources-- so there

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:22.480
are a number of people
involved in this.

00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:23.710
There's the customer.

00:45:23.710 --> 00:45:24.540
There's a partner.

00:45:24.540 --> 00:45:25.352
There's Google.

00:45:25.352 --> 00:45:26.560
And there are others as well.

00:45:26.560 --> 00:45:28.530
We may have analysts
in a specific domain.

00:45:28.530 --> 00:45:30.840
We may have consultants
that have done this

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:33.030
before that we're bringing
in to work with us.

00:45:33.030 --> 00:45:34.980
It's really important
to understand

00:45:34.980 --> 00:45:38.190
who these people are, what role
they are, because another thing

00:45:38.190 --> 00:45:40.691
that we see that happens
is we start a migration.

00:45:40.691 --> 00:45:42.190
We're like, OK,
we've got this team.

00:45:42.190 --> 00:45:43.523
They're going to do a migration.

00:45:43.523 --> 00:45:46.539
And suddenly there's this
reason like, for example, we

00:45:46.539 --> 00:45:49.080
don't have a partner in place
to actually help us and give us

00:45:49.080 --> 00:45:51.270
the breadth that we need,
or we don't have one

00:45:51.270 --> 00:45:52.910
of the data experts in place.

00:45:52.910 --> 00:45:54.660
And we don't actually
understand the stuff

00:45:54.660 --> 00:45:55.821
that we're now migrating.

00:45:55.821 --> 00:45:58.320
And we started the migration,
and now we're like three weeks

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:00.486
and we've got a block and
that person is on holiday,

00:46:00.486 --> 00:46:03.850
and now we have to wait, and,
you know, it's fun and games.

00:46:03.850 --> 00:46:06.150
So making sure that we
understand the resources,

00:46:06.150 --> 00:46:08.760
and who they are, and getting
them lined up timely for this

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:11.760
is really important as
well to be successful.

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:15.360
Commit and execute-- so there
are some things here you need

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:19.080
to think about-- security,
data locality, the cost,

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:20.484
licensing if you're--

00:46:20.484 --> 00:46:21.900
there may be some
of the stuff you

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:25.084
want to use the third-party
stuff from partners

00:46:25.084 --> 00:46:27.250
and you need to bring your
own license, for example.

00:46:27.250 --> 00:46:28.708
So think about all
of these things.

00:46:28.708 --> 00:46:30.960
And when you're about
to execute on this,

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:33.450
make sure that anything
that we execute on

00:46:33.450 --> 00:46:35.430
addresses those concerns.

00:46:35.430 --> 00:46:37.260
Otherwise, we go through
another iteration

00:46:37.260 --> 00:46:39.100
with everyone kind of
standing around saying, hey,

00:46:39.100 --> 00:46:41.100
but the security doesn't
live up to what it was.

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:43.125
The security is going
to be really good.

00:46:43.125 --> 00:46:45.000
But we need to make sure
everyone understands

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:46.350
that the security is
going to be really good

00:46:46.350 --> 00:46:48.420
and has had their chance
to explore that and get

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:51.360
information about it.

00:46:51.360 --> 00:46:53.510
And the next thing-- you've
just delivered an MVP.

00:46:53.510 --> 00:46:54.910
Show everyone.

00:46:54.910 --> 00:46:56.660
Get everyone excited.

00:46:56.660 --> 00:46:58.700
Let them see that it
solves their use case.

00:46:58.700 --> 00:47:03.200
And then get the next one
lined up and reiterate.

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:06.080
And just keep doing this
until everything's migrated.

00:47:06.080 --> 00:47:08.000
And you know, you'll be happy.

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:09.770
Your end users will be happy.

00:47:09.770 --> 00:47:14.180
And you'll be asking for new
product features on BigQuery.

00:47:14.180 --> 00:47:15.914
So that's it.

